    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f8bee545-8d3d-5a42-ab25-7119b17c6f69",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3x+(-1)y}{4}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,3)$,$(-1,-1)$,$(-3,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{3(-2)+(-1)(3)}{4}=- \\frac{9}{4}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=\\frac{3(-1)+(-1)(-1)}{4}=- \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-1)}=\\frac{3(-3)+(-1)(-1)}{4}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/1d/1dd1a2bade9c53b136f6f66e3b3affe033a51c25dee9eb800412f81ab63c68f1.png)",
