      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+(3)y}{3}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,3)$,$(-1,2)$,$(-2,3)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,3)}=\\frac{2(2)+(3)(3)}{3}=\\frac{13}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=\\frac{2(-1)+(3)(2)}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(-2)+(3)(3)}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/f9/f99bc2a1d6becc2fe69d65bc6d9b83412e4f6f225059ba65953fbf5c84cca41d.png)",
